[84.876539, "o", "\u001b[29A\u001b[1G\u001b[K\u001b[38;5;243m  \u2502  \u001b[39m\u001b[2m(\u001b[22m\u001b[38;5;35m+20\u001b[39m\u001b[2m total)\u001b[22m\r\r\n\u001b[3G\u001b[K\u001b[2m\u2570\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u001b[22m\r\r\n\u001b[1G\u001b[K\r\r\n\u001b[1G\u001b[KNow let's run the tests to find the bug:\r\r\n\u001b[1G\u001b[K\r\r\n\u001b[1G\u001b[K\u001b[38;5;208m\u25b6 \u001b[39m \u001b[38;5;203m\u2717 \u001b[39m\u001b[2mRan bash(============================= test session star...) 0.2s\u001b[22m\r\r\n\u001b[E\u001b[1G\u001b[KPerfect! I found 4 failing tests. Let me analyze the issues:\r\r\n\u001b[E\u001b[1G\u001b[K1. \u001b[1mZero-length intervals at same point\u001b[22m: overlaps(2, 2, 2, 2) fails because the condition 2 < 2 is\r\r\n\u001b[1G\u001b[KFalse (we're using strict inequality <)\r\r\n\u001b[1G\u001b[K2. \u001b[1mReversed arguments\u001b[22m: The function doesn't handle cases where start > end (reversed intervals)\r\r\n\u001b[E\u001b[1G\u001b[KThe current logic uses strict inequality, which fails when intervals touch at a single point or when\r\r\n\u001b[1G\u001b[Kdealing with zero-length intervals. Also, the function assumes intervals are well-formed (start \u2264\r\r\n\u001b[1G\u001b[Kend).\r\r\n\u001b[E\u001b[1G\u001b[KLet me think about the semantics. For zero-length intervals at the same point, should they be\r\r\n\u001b[1G\u001b[Kconsidered as overlapping? Mathematically, a point [2,2] and another point [2,2] could be considered\r\r\n\u001b[1G\u001b[Kas overlapping (they share a common point). For reversed intervals, we need to normalize them first.\r\r\n\u001b[1G\u001b[K\r\r\n\u001b[1G\u001b[KLet me fix the `i